  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: \nStorm chances stay with us into the weekend with best chances in\nthe mountains. Some severe/flooding possible mainly through late\nWednesday into Thursday morning.\n\n\nThe overall large scale upper level and mid level pattern have\nremained largely unchanged since Monday across the area. An\nupper level ridge in the western Conus and slight upper level\ntroughing across the Northeast and Mid-Atlantic states in the\neastern Conus will continue to allow MCS disturbances to\ntraverse east along the northern periphery of the ridge for\ntoday, Thursday, and possibly Friday. The upper level ridge \nlooks to nudge far enough east by Saturday that the train of \nrepeated MCS should come to an end; however, with moisture \nremaining high across the region, daily showers and thunderstorm\nchances will still be possible. \n\nFor today, CAM model guidance has thunderstorm coverage\nremaining minimal today; however, global model guidance\ncontinues to bring in a possible MCS through the late evening \nhours today. The latest NBM guidance had low PoPs for today, but\nhave opted to increase them slightly to show this chance of\nadditional thunderstorm coverage during the late afternoon and\nearly evening hours today. With thunderstorm coverage hinging on\ncurrent upstream convection, the overall forecast will continue\nto remain uncertain in this northwest flow pattern. \n\nWith elevated deep layer shear remaining over the region, the\npotential for organized severe weather across parts of the \nregion will continue today. At least isolated storm development\nwill be possible across favored terrain areas, with modest \nwest- northwesterly flow aloft supporting potential for outflow-\ndriven cells/clusters with a threat of wind damage. Any features\nfavorably timed with the diurnal cycle (such as a persistent \nstorm cluster or MCV) could result in a greater coverage of \nwind-damage potential, but confidence is low.",
